          "urls": [
            {
              "display_url": "x.com/jessethanley/s…",
              "end": 298,
              "expanded_url": "https://twitter.com/jessethanley/status/2074969590350901284",
              "start": 275,
              "url": "https://t.co/1qR5NsJZBL"
            }
          ]
        },
        "id": "2075011146646691996",
        "lang": "en",
        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {
            "mentions": [
              {
                "end": 77,
                "id": "1695890961094909952",
                "start": 67,
                "username": "cursor_ai"
              },
              {
                "end": 89,
                "id": "2772253272",
                "start": 83,
                "username": "Bento"
              },
              {
                "end": 443,
                "id": "2832427459",
                "start": 436,
                "username": "poteto"
              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "Okay so here is my take after merging in ~12 PRs from Grok 4.5 via @cursor_ai into @Bento this morning: \n\nIt is good and safe to setup as default but only if you have guardrails/not default harness setup. \n\nThis is the first model from Grok/Cursor you can use for day to day coding, as you were with Opus or GPT 5.5, but you have to have your harness spec'd out to keep it focused and on task. \n\nFor Cursor users, this means installing @poteto pstack and using the skills to kick off agents (\"/poteto-mode build X feature\"). \n\nIf you do that it crushes just as the other models do. \n\nIf you don't it loses the plot. \n\nExample from a PR this morning: customer reported an issue with a timezone picker (kyiv/kiev). \n\nWith /poteto-mode (pstack): 1 file change, a few lines. Worked.\n\nWithout skills: 15 file changes, dedicated Bento::Kyiv classes (????), just a mess. Worked but absolute slop. \n\nI also find that it has a habit of refactoring aggressively which is sometimes nice but can be annoying as often I just want very targeted fixes. \n\nAgain, spending time on skills and playbooks seems to be the key to making this model work and stopping it from doing this. \n\nI also imagine the ponytail project would also help here greatly. \n\nIt also says \"code judo\" a lot. \n\nTAKEAWAY: Spend 1 hour today playing with pstack or learning what it is and making your own version then use Grok 4.5 day to day!"
        },
        "public_metrics": {
          "bookmark_count": 55,
          "impression_count": 14070,
          "like_count": 77,
          "quote_count": 1,
          "reply_count": 12,
          "retweet_count": 1
        },
        "referenced_tweets": [
          {
            "id": "2074969590350901284",
            "type": "quoted"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Okay so here is my take after merging in ~12 PRs from Grok 4.5 via @cursor_ai into @Bento this morning: \n\nIt is good and safe to setup as default but only if you have guardrails/not default harness setup. \n\nThis is the first model from Grok/Cursor you can use for day to day https://t.co/1qR5NsJZBL"
      },
